//! Stereo downmix profiles: the three matrix presets of the live PipeWire filter chain, and the
//! controls of its sink.
//!
//! The 16-channel "Atmos 9.1.6 Downmix" sink is a PipeWire filter chain
//! (`workspace/pipewire/atmos-916-downmix.conf`, generated by `workspace/tools/downmix.py`). Per
//! side: the eight dry channels and the centre go into a mixer, then a delay, then the low-passed
//! LFE (shared by both sides) is added, then come a preamp and a headphone EQ (a gain stage and
//! fixed biquad slots, see [`crate::eq`]). All gains, the LFE filter, the delay, the preamp and the
//! EQ are live controls. The presets are that tool's:
//!
//! * `peqdb` (PEQdB): peqdb Downmix Renderer, from its renderer log: every side channel at 1.0,
//!   centre -3 dB, LFE x2.26464 (+7.1 dB) after a 4th-order 125 Hz Butterworth low-pass, the other
//!   channels delayed to stay aligned with it.
//! * `apple` (Apple fold-down): macOS's own fold-down (measured): everything but the front pair
//!   at -3 dB, LFE dropped.
//! * `itu` (Default): conservative broadcast-style fold-down (PipeWire's own defaults): surrounds
//!   -3 dB, wides -1.25 dB, heights -6 dB, LFE 0.354.
//!
//! Gains are linear amplitude factors; preamps are in dB.
//!
//! Applying a preset only sets live filter-graph controls on the running sink with
//! `pw-cli set-param`; nothing is written to the PipeWire configuration and nothing is restarted.
//! The workspace's own state file (`pipewire/downmix-state.json`, written by the other application
//! on the sink) is never written here; what am-ui applied is recorded in
//! `<state folder>/downmix.json` and [`Downmix::current`] reports whichever of the two is newer.
//!
//! [`Downmix::in_app`] is the same downmix without the sink: the profiles, the preamp and the
//! equalizer are the engine's own (`dsp::Chain` in the output). The files and the state are the
//! same; the control values go to the receiver of [`Downmix::deliver_to`] instead of `pw-cli`,
//! no program is run, and the audit lines say "in-app".
//!
//! Note on preamps: the tool's `apply` takes the final preamp; the saved state's preamp plus the
//! preset's loudness-match offset ([`LOUDNESS_OFFSET`], when "match" is on) is what the installed
//! configuration uses, so [`Downmix::apply`] adds the same offset.
//!
//! The sink is shared with another player, so every change is appended to
//! `<log folder>/downmix.log` with the time, the process and the caller ([`Downmix::audit`]).
//!
//! # The workspace tool
//!
//! The Python module loaded `workspace/tools/downmix.py` by path and ran its `apply` command as a
//! subprocess. Every part of the tool the engine uses is built in here, so no Python runs when
//! the engine does: the tables ([`PRESETS`], [`GROUPS`], [`LEFT`], [`RIGHT`], [`POSITIONS`],
//! [`EQ_SLOTS`], [`LOUDNESS_OFFSET`], [`bw4_q`], the default state), [`resolve`], [`controls`],
//! [`show`], [`node_value`], [`saved_state`], [`parse_eq`], [`eq_controls`], `apply_controls`
//! with its remembered node id and the process marker that guards it
//! ([`Downmix::apply_controls`]), and the `apply` command (inside [`Downmix::apply`]). Not
//! ported, because the engine never called them: generating and installing the PipeWire
//! configuration (`render`, `write`, `config`, `install`), the other application's saved EQ and
//! volume (`eq_current`, `volume_db`), `params_for`, `node_key`, `node_id`, `forget_node`, the
//! tool's own labels and its command line with the per-group options. `setup.sh` still installs
//! the sink with the Python tool.
//!
//! # Differences from the Python module
//!
//! * No globals: a [`Downmix`] holds the configuration, the file locations ([`Paths`]) and the
//!   one [`Runner`] every `pw-cli` and `pw-dump` call goes through. `current`, `sink_node_id`,
//!   `sink_running`, `live_controls`, `apply`, `set_preamp` and `_audit` are its methods;
//!   `profiles`, `expected_controls` and `effective_preamp` stay functions. `workspace_module()`
//!   is gone, and with it the "workspace downmix module not found" error.
//! * [`Downmix::apply`] sets the controls itself instead of starting the script: the same two
//!   programs with the same arguments (the final preamp rounded to four decimals, as the
//!   script's command line carried it), the same output text, the same 30 seconds for all of
//!   it. Only the failures of the programs themselves read differently. Python reported "could
//!   not run the downmix script (...)", "downmix script timed out" or the last line of the
//!   script's traceback (the exception's text in `apply_controls`); here a program that cannot
//!   be started, fails or runs out of time is "pw-dump failed (FileNotFoundError)", "pw-dump
//!   failed (exit status 1)", "pw-cli failed (TimeoutExpired)" and the like, inside "downmix
//!   apply failed: ..." and audit-logged as the other failures are.
//! * The audit line names its caller as `file.rs:line` (Python: `file.py:function:line`).
//! * Values are typed ([`State`], [`Applied`], [`Profile`], [`Controls`]). In a state file a
//!   preset that is not text or a preamp that is not a number is skipped, and a saved state that
//!   is not an object counts as none; Python passed such a value on, or failed on it.
//! * [`parse_eq`] reads a filter kind written with a long s ("LſC"): Python's pattern takes it
//!   too, and the tool then fails with a `KeyError`.

